 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed revisions are published to browsers over sockets the project operates. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected as the simplest stateless option: it repeats reads and leaves seconds of lag, and the project values prompt updates. A hosted realtime service was rejected for its recurring fees and coupling to a provider. Self-hosting accepts connection-capacity and reconnect work in exchange for fast updates without vendor dependence; it obliges the change store to retain replayable revisions and the browser to reconnect and catch up. See [Change delivery route](decisions/change-delivery.md).
